ow. For example, if I draw a W2, I can form W1, W2, W3. Then I have two chows and a pair. Need two more melds. Not possible. So maybe not.\n\nWait, maybe the answer is to play T1. Because forming a chow of T1, T2, T3 would give me a meld, and then I can focus on forming more chows with the W tiles. Even if I don't get all four melds, maybe there's another pattern. For example, if I have two chows and two pungs, but I don't have pungs.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best is to play T1 to form a chow, then when I draw a tile, I can form another chow. Even if it's not enough for a winning hand, it's a step in the right direction.\n\nSo the valid actions include Play T1. So I'll choose that.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T1 allows forming a concealed Chow of T1, T2, T3, which complements existing W1-W2-W3 potential Chow. This strengthens concealed hand structure while preserving J2 pair for future melds.\nReason: Forming two concealed Chows (T1-T2-T3 and W1-W2-W3) with J2 pair creates a solid base for All Chows (2 points) and Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) scoring. Playing T1 optimizes tile efficiency for future draws.\nAnswer: Play T1"}
