ent hand is:\n\nMeld: T1-T2-T3 (Chi)\n\nHand tiles: W1, W2, W3, W7, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J2, J3.\n\nWait, that's 11 tiles. So with the meld, total is 14. So I need to arrange these 11 tiles into three more melds and a pair. Let's see:\n\nPossible melds:\n\n- Chi W1-W2-W3 (uses three tiles, leaving 8 tiles: W7, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J2, J3)\n\nThen, from those 8 tiles, need two more melds and a pair.\n\nLooking at the remaining tiles: W7, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J2, J3.\n\nPossible options:\n\n- Pair J3 (if another J3 is available) but I have only one. So maybe not.\n\n- Pung F1-F2-F3? No, I have one of each. So can't form a pung.\n\n- Pair F1, F2, or F3? No, only one each.\n\n- Pair J2 and J3? No, only one each.\n\n- Maybe form a pair of J2 and J3? No, they are different.\n\nWait, maybe I can form a pung of J3 if I can get another. But I have only one. So perhaps this approach isn't working.\n\nAlternative approach: Maybe don't form the Chi of W1-W2-W3, and instead look for other melds.\n\nLooking at the remaining tiles: W1, W2, W3, W7, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J2, J3.\n\nIf I can form a pair with J2 and J3, but they are different. Not helpful. Maybe a pair of J3 and J3? No.\n\nAlternatively, maybe form a pung of F1, F2, F3? No, I have one of each. So no.\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Maybe I need to discard a tile to allow forming a pair. Let's see:\n\nIf I discard W7, the remaining tiles are W1, W2, W3, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J2, J3. That's 10 tiles. Then, form the Chi of W1-W2-W3, leaving 7 tiles: B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J2, J3. Need two melds and a pair. Maybe form a pair of J3 and J3? No. Or maybe form a pung of F1-F2-F3? No. Or maybe form a pung of J2-J2-J2? No. Not possible. So this doesn't help.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard J2, leaving W1, W2, W3, W7, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J3. Form the Chi of W1-W2-W3, leaving W7, B1, B4, F1, F2, F3, J3. Still no obvious melds.\n\nWait, maybe I should not form the Chi of W1-W2-W3 and instead look for other combinations. For example, if I can form a 