but I only have two. So no. Or a pair of W1 and another Chi. But I already have the Chi of W1-W2-W3. Then, the pair would be W1. But that would require two W1s. I have two, so that's possible. So if I form a Chi of W1-W2-W3 and a pair of W1. Then, that's two melds. But I need four. So still two more.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Maybe I should look for the best possible discard that doesn't block my potential melds. The W2 is part of the Chi of W1-W2-W3. If I discard W2, I can't form that Chi. So that's bad. Similarly, W3 is part of the Chi. So discarding W3 is bad. Discarding W1 is okay if I have another one. So discarding one W1 would leave me with one, which can be part of the Chi. So maybe that's the way to go.\n\nSo if I discard a W1, then I can form the Chi of W1-W2-W3. Then, the remaining W1 could be part of a pair. But I need two more melds. The J3 pair is there. So that's two melds. The remaining tiles are W7, B4, F1, F3, J2. These are all single. So I need to form two more melds. Not possible. So this approach is not working.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. The melds of the previous player include a Kong of F4 and a Chi of T4-T5-T6. The next player has a Pung of F1. The player across has a Chi of W3. So maybe there are tiles that others have, which I can use. But since I can't draw, I have to discard.\n\nAnother thought: Maybe my hand is not winnable, and I need to discard a tile to improve my chances. The safest discard is one that doesn't help others. For example, if I discard B4, which is a single, and no one else has a Chi that needs it. Or discard F3, which is a single. Let's see the unshown tiles for F3: F3 has 1 left. So if I discard F3, someone might need it. Similarly for J3: there are 3 left. So discarding J3 might be safe.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard W7. It's a single, and no one else has a Chi that needs it. The previous player has a Chi of T4-T5-T6, so maybe W7 is not needed.\n\nSo considering all this, the best move might be to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld. Between B4, F1, F3, J2, J3, W7. Let's pick one that's least useful.\n\nIf I discard J3, I lose a potential pair, but if I keep it, I can have a pair. So maybe not the best. If I discard W7, it's a single. So maybe that's the best option. Let's check the valid actions: Play W7 is an option. So playing W7 would remove a single tile that's not part of any meld. That seems safe.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding W7 is optimal as it removes a lone tile not part of any potential meld, preserving the possibility to form a Chi of W1-W2-W3 and a pair of J3. Other discards risk blocking melds or leaving more problematic tiles.]\nReason: [W7 is a single tile with no immediate use in melds. Discarding it maintains flexibility for forming the Chi of W1-W2-W3 and leaves more honor tiles (J3 pair) for potential scoring. Other options like W2 or W3 would disrupt the Chi formation, while B4 or F1/F3 are less critical to future melds.]\nAnswer: Play W7"}
